From c14b9b6a651b35a24df445d09e7f7e8116d1c51e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vali0004 <diorcheats.vali@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 21:21:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Initial support for the Broadcom VCE (video coding engine)

The VCE is the fixed-function video block in BCM283x.  It is a separate
processor from the VideoCore IV VPU, with its own ISA: one fixed 32-bit
little-endian instruction word, 64 register fields, and separate code and
data address spaces.

  31      26 25  24 23    18 17    12 11             0
 +----------+------+--------+--------+----------------+
 |  opcode  | cond |   rd   |   rs   |      imm       |
 +----------+------+--------+--------+----------------+

Microcode for it is embedded in the VPU's .text inside start.elf; twenty
programs are extractable, totalling 31516 instructions, which is the
corpus the operation tables here were checked against.  The
codec-license-hash program was also executed on real silicon and
reproduces its expected outputs exactly, which validates the semantics.

Operations whose meaning is not established are named unk_0bXXXXXX and
emit a vce_unknown p-code op rather than a guess: a wrong mnemonic or
wrong semantics is worse than an honest gap.
