+# The dma* family programs a transfer one field at a time and then kicks it.
+# Across all 20 microcode blobs the four address opcodes always carry imm=0 and
+# `rd` in 0..3, which is a slot selector rather than a destination register -
+# nothing ever reads the register these would name.  The address itself comes
+# from `rs`.
+#
+#   dmafr / dmato    set the remote (external memory) address
+#   dmatdl / dmafdl  set the local (data RAM) address
+#
+# The two directions pair exactly: a read is `dmafr` then `dmatdl` (from
+# remote, to data-local) and a write is `dmato` then `dmafdl`.  The corpus
+# counts confirm it - 56 dmafr against 56 dmatdl, 28 dmato against 28 dmafdl,
+# with no unpaired occurrence.  A small value is latched through
+# `sendc blank, r17, 0` just before each pair, which looks like the transfer
+# length.  `dmarun` then starts it, and is invariant: always
+# `dmarun blank, blank, 0x1`, both register fields blank, in all 48 uses.
+#
+# All of this is corpus inference; none of it has been checked on hardware, so
+# every one of these still lowers to an opaque vce_dma rather than to memory
+# semantics that could be wrong.
+:dmafr^CC rd, RS, B is op=16 & CC & rd & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	vce_dma(RS, B);
+}
+
+:dmato^CC rd, RS, B is op=17 & CC & rd & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	vce_dma(RS, B);
+}
+
+:dmatdl^CC rd, RS, B is op=18 & CC & rd & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	vce_dma(RS, B);
+}
+
+:dmafdl^CC rd, RS, B is op=19 & CC & rd & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	vce_dma(RS, B);
+}
+
+:dmacl^CC rd, RS, B is op=27 & CC & rd & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	vce_dma(RS, B);
+}
+
+:zero^CC RD, RS, B is op=24 & CC & RD & RS & B {
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	result(RD, 0:4);
+}
