+# EVERY BRANCH HAS ONE DELAY SLOT: the instruction after it executes whether or
+# not the branch is taken.  Measured on hardware with the vce_branchland probe -
+# a `j` followed by per-word markers shows the next word running for every
+# target, forwards and backwards, including a jump backwards over itself.
+#
+# Without `delayslot(1)` Ghidra believes that instruction never runs, so every
+# delay slot in the program - and anything reachable only through one - is
+# reported as an unreachable block.  That is where the hundreds of "Removing
+# unreachable block" warnings came from, and why decompiled routines collapsed
+# to almost nothing.  The corpus puts real work in these slots, `sendc` and
+# `or` and stores, so they are not padding that can be ignored.
+
+# Branch.  The target is `rs | imm`, the same OR-addressing the loads use - NOT
+# the immediate alone.  The immediate is a byte offset within the program, so
+# `j.ne blank, blank, 0x2f0` lands on the instruction at 0x2f0.
+#
+# `rs` is load-bearing and ignoring it was badly wrong.  This machine's return
+# instruction is `j blank, <reg>, 0x0` - fourteen of the twenty microcode blobs
+# end with `j blank, last, 0x0`, jumping back to an address the caller left
+# behind - and measured on hardware with vce_jumpreg, landing is exact through
+# `last` and through an ordinary register alike.  With `rs` dropped, every one
+# of those returns decoded as `goto 0`: control flow folded back to the program
+# entry, everything past a return became unreachable, and whole functions
+# collapsed into `do { } while(true)`.
+#
+# `blank` exports a constant 0, so the common `j blank, blank, imm` still folds
+# to a plain direct branch and only genuinely register-relative jumps become
+# computed ones.
+# THE LOW TWO BITS OF A BRANCH IMMEDIATE ARE NOT PART OF THE ADDRESS.
+#
+# 2606 of the 4578 rs=blank branches in the corpus - 57% - have imm&3 != 0,
+# which cannot be an error.  Taking the immediate literally produces targets
+# like 0x69 that Ghidra will not decode at, because they overlap the aligned
+# instruction below: 71 of h263_decode_mbloop's 1098 words failed to decode for
+# exactly this reason, and that is where the "Instruction at 0x55 overlaps
+# 0x54" warning and most of the unreachable blocks came from.  Masking the low
+# bits takes those 71 failures to 3.
+#
+# It also settles an old hardware result that looked like a contradiction:
+# `j 0x5` was measured as "running nothing but the delay slot".  Masked, its
+# target is 0x4 = word 1, which IS the delay slot - so landing there and not
+# branching at all cannot be told apart from outside.
+#
+# What the low bits MEAN is unknown - an annul flag, a hint, a mode select.
+# Masking them is what the corpus demands; it is not a claim to understand them.
+jt_addr: rel is imm [ rel = imm & 0xffc; ] { export *:4 rel; }
+JTarget: jt_addr is jt_addr { export jt_addr; }
+JOff:    imm is imm { export *[const]:4 imm; }
+
+# Splitting the register-relative forms on whether the offset is zero: an
+# offset of zero is the return idiom, anything else is a computed jump.
+immzero:    is imm=0     { }
+immnonzero: is imm       { }
+
+:j rd, RS, JTarget is op=28 & cond=0 & rsnum=63 & rd & RS & JTarget {
+	delayslot(1);
+	goto JTarget;
+}
+
+# `j blank, <reg>, 0x0` is the RETURN, and modelling it as a plain computed
+# goto is worse than useless: Ghidra cannot recover the target, so flow
+# analysis stops dead there and every block after it is reported unreachable -
+# whole functions decompile as an empty `do { } while(true)`.
+#
+# It really is a return.  Fourteen of the twenty microcode blobs end with
+# `j blank, last, 0x0`, jumping back to an address the caller left behind, and
+# vce_jumpreg confirmed on hardware that landing is exact through `last` and
+# through an ordinary register alike.  Telling Ghidra so lets it close the
+# function instead of giving up on it.
+:j rd, RS, JOff is op=28 & cond=0 & rsnum=0 & immzero & rd & RS & JOff {
+	local t:4; eaddr(t, RS, JOff);
+	delayslot(1);
+	return [t];
+}
+
+# A register-relative jump with a non-zero offset is a genuine computed branch,
+# not a return, so it stays a goto.
+:j rd, RS, JOff is op=28 & cond=0 & rsnum!=63 & rsnum!=0 & rd & RS & JOff {
+	local t:4; eaddr(t, RS, JOff);
+	delayslot(1);
+	goto [t];
+}
+
+# The conditional forms.  NOTE, and this is measured but deliberately not
+# modelled here: on real silicon a `j` ALWAYS transfers, to
+# `rs | (predicate ? imm : 0)` - the predicate gates the immediate into the
+# address rather than gating the branch, so a conditional that fails with
+# rs = blank lands on word 0 rather than falling through.  Modelling that would
+# turn every conditional into a two-way computed goto and make the listing
+# unreadable, and the corpus is plainly written expecting fall-through, so the
+# straightforward reading is kept.  If a decompiled routine seems to fall
+# through where the hardware would not, this is why.
+:j^CC rd, RS, JTarget is op=28 & CC & cond!=0 & rsnum=63 & rd & RS & JTarget {
+	delayslot(1);
+	if (CC) goto JTarget;
+}
+
+:j^CC rd, RS, JOff is op=28 & CC & cond!=0 & rsnum=0 & immzero & rd & RS & JOff {
+	delayslot(1);
+	# SLEIGH has no conditional computed goto - `if (CC) goto [t]` is
+	# "invalid dynamic target used in goto destination" - so branch over it,
+	# which is the idiom the rest of this file uses for predication.
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	local t:4; eaddr(t, RS, JOff);
+	return [t];
+}
+
+:j^CC rd, RS, JOff is op=28 & CC & cond!=0 & rsnum!=63 & rsnum!=0 & rd & RS & JOff {
+	delayslot(1);
+	if (!CC) goto inst_next;
+	local t:4; eaddr(t, RS, JOff);
+	goto [t];
+}
